## Part 3: Where do we Go from here?

Modern foundations

.image image/spacer.png 220 1

.image image/gopher5.png

## Go: Making software engineering simpler

So up until now, this is all standard stuff...but what sets Go apart in today's landscape?

- **Scalability**: Designed for modern multi-core systems through goroutines.
- **Channels:** Concurrent thread communication for scaling with safety and clarity.
- **Minimal OOP**: No inheritance, no function overloading, no hidden constructors.
- **Error Handling:** functions return an error value instead of throwing exceptions.
- **Standard Library:** a robust, all-in-one toolkit with fewer external dependencies.

.background image/gopher_eyes_blue.png 

## Harnessing concurrency

- **Concurrency ≠ Parallelism**: managing multiple tasks at once vs executing them at the exact same time.
- **Goroutines**: Lightweight threads managed by the Go runtime.
- **Easy Concurrency**: Launch hundreds of thousands without the overhead of traditional OS threads.

.play -numbers -edit code/concurrency1.go /START OMIT/,/END OMIT/

## Channels
- **Send and Receive**: Use `chan` to communicate between goroutines.
- **Strongly Typed**: they are typed by the values they send/receive.
- **Synchronize**: syncs the two goroutines, per [Hoare's CSP model](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Communicating_sequential_processes).

.play -numbers -edit code/sum3.go /START OMIT/,/END OMIT/

## Interfaces 

- Defines behavior, not implementation.
- Enables polymorphism without inheritance.
- Offers flexibility and interchangeability.

.play -numbers -edit code/interface1.go /START OMIT/,/END OMIT/

## Complex interface example

.play -numbers -edit code/interface2.go /START OMIT/,/END OMIT/

## Generics

**Why Generics?** (Go 1.18+)
- Type safety without duplicating code per type.
- Reduces boilerplate.

.play -numbers -edit code/generics.go  /START OMIT/,/END OMIT/

## Structs and composition

- No "class" in Go; composition over inheritance.
- Embed one struct in another to promote its fields/methods.
- Embedding isn't subtyping.

.play -numbers -edit code/struct2.go /START OMIT/,/END OMIT/

## Error handling in depth

*The Go Way:* explicit error handling over exceptions.
- Check errors immediately, handle them, or propagate them up.
- Any type with an `Error() string` method satisfies the `error` interface.
- The "indented" code block is the error path, making scanning easier.

.play -numbers -edit code/error1.go /START OMIT/,/END OMIT/

## Defer, Panic, and Recover
`defer`: Schedule a function to run after the surrounding function returns.
- Useful for cleanup actions like closing files, releasing locks, etc.
- `defers` stack, executing in LIFO order.

.play -numbers -edit code/defer1.go /START OMIT/,/END OMIT/

`panic` and `recover`:
- panic: Stops normal execution, similar to throwing an exception.
- recover: Called in a defer, catches a panic so the program continues.

.image image/gopher4.png

## The standard library

- **Documentation**: comprehensive docs and examples, all at [pkg.go.dev](https://pkg.go.dev).
- **Extensive**: A vast collection encompassing various domains.
- **Performant**: solid performance, though a few packages (e.g. `encoding/json`) trade speed for simplicity.
- **Reliability**: stable, mature, and vetted by years of production use worldwide.
- **Backward Compatibility**: Go commits to keeping old code working - boring, on purpose.

.image image/Go-Logo_Blue.png 250 _

## Go and the AI-infused SDLC

**Working in Go's favor:**
- **Fast feedback loop**: `go test`/`vet`/`fmt` + `govulncheck` give a cheap, free signal.
- **Low ambiguity**: few keywords, explicit errors narrow "plausible but wrong" output.
- **Language stability**: Go 1 compatibility means training data rarely goes stale - boring, on purpose.

**Working against it:**
- **Smaller training corpus**: benchmarks rank Go mid-pack, behind Python/JS/TS.
- **Newer idioms underused**: generics, `range`-over-func often skipped.

.image image/UNT_green.png 1 400

**Bottom line:** Go helps AI *iterate* on code more than *write* it right the first time.

## Part 4: Go(ld)-Standard

Ensuring software quality

.image image/spacer.png 220 1

.image image/gopher5.png

## Go's emphasis on software quality
  
- **Safety Through Testing:** Go's built-in testing suite encourages robust code.
- **Coverage:** Assess how well your tests cover your codebase.
- **Fuzzing:** Identify vulnerabilities by feeding random input.
- **Performance Insights:** Diagnose performance bottlenecks with in-depth profiling.
- **Optimize with Confidence:** Quantify and diff performance over time or changes.
- **Consistent Tooling:** every tool runs through the centralized `go` command.

.background image/gopher_eyes_green.png

## Built-in testing

**Overview:**
- Go's built-in testing framework.
- Utilizes the `testing` package.
- Lives in a `_test.go` file; test functions are named `TestXxx(t *testing.T)`.

```go
func TestFunctionName(t *testing.T) {
    if got := YourFunction(); got != "expectedValue" {
        t.Errorf("got %v, want expectedValue", got)
    }
}
```
**Usage:** `go test`

[GoLang Unit Testing and Mock Testing Tutorial](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XQzTUa9LPU8)

## Built-in coverage

**Overview:**
- Measure the tested portion of your code.
- Identify untested parts of your codebase.
- Whole-program/integration coverage via `go build -cover`.

**Commands:**
- Run tests with coverage: `go test -cover`
- View detailed coverage: 
  ```
  go test -coverprofile=coverage.out
  go tool cover -html=coverage.out
  ```

[GopherCon UK 2021: Eleni Fragkiadaki - Test coverage in Go, the whole package](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=JTLB7j8M85A)  

## Built-in fuzzing

**Overview:**
- Coverage-guided mutation of a seed corpus to find edge cases and potential bugs.
- Has become popular for finding [security vulnerabilities](https://owasp.org/www-community/Fuzzing) in sensitive code.
- [Introduced in Go 1.18](https://go.dev/doc/tutorial/fuzz).

```go
func FuzzFunctionName(f *testing.F) {
    f.Add("initial input")
    f.Fuzz(func(t *testing.T, input string) {
        // Use input in some way
    })
}
```
**Usage:** `go test -fuzz=FuzzFunctionName`

[GopherCon 2022: Katie Hockman - Fuzz Testing Made Easy](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7KWPiRq3ZYI)

## Built-in profiling

- Diagnose performance bottlenecks: CPU, memory, and block profilers.
- Blank-import `_ "net/http/pprof"`, then `http://localhost:PORT/debug/pprof/`

Flame graph from `go tool pprof -http=:PORT` 
- width is CPU time, i.e.  *wide boxes are hot*

.image image/pprof_flamegraph.png 180 _

- [Profiling & Optimizing in Go / Brad Fitzpatrick](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=xxDZuPEgbBU)
- [GopherCon 2019: Two Go Programs, Three Different Profiling Techniques](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=nok0aYiGiYA)

## Built-in benchmarking

**Overview:**
- Assess your functions' performance.
- Use the `testing` package.

```go
func BenchmarkFunctionName(b *testing.B) {
    for b.Loop() {
        YourFunction()
    }
}
```
**Usage:** `go test -bench=.`
- Compare runs with `benchstat` ([golang.org/x/perf/cmd/benchstat](https://pkg.go.dev/golang.org/x/perf/cmd/benchstat)).

[Go Live: The Art of Performance - Profiling & Benchmarking in Go](https://youtu.be/R_C2KqmlqY8?t=81)

